@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+# Isolated capacity prototype
+
+This is preparation only. These files are archived as an unapplied experiment patch. They are not imported by the deployed Worker or applied to the production database. No schedules, participant writes or real SMTP tests were made by this prototype. Separate model-review attempts are recorded in issue.md.
+
+## What is implemented
+
+- One durable job row with a five-minute exclusive lease.
+- Recipient cursor advances fairly after a complete scan.
+- Candidate pages contain at most 16 people. All candidates at the captured joined-time cutoff are scanned, not only the first page.
+- Only top three suggestions persist between pages; photo/contact/link fields are excluded from candidate query responses.
+- Final queue insertion and job completion are atomic and deduplicated.
+- Scheduled AI mode fails closed before database/SMTP work when a Gemini key is present. No chunked model ranking is substituted.
+- Separate flushOne entry point processes at most one queued message and retains existing quota/failure rules. Candidate availability reads only the proposed IDs.
+
+## Evidence
+
+The final combined suite has 43 passing tests, including 14 bounded-job tests, five snapshot tests and the existing domain/server/matching/SMTP tests. The 1,000-candidate fixture requires 63 invocations, scans all 1,000 candidates, and chooses the strongest candidates from the final page. Maximum observed digest SQL requests per invocation: 7 in this fixture (startup plus completion is structurally at most 8). One match-mail delivery uses 14 SQL calls plus mocked SMTP. Tests cover overlap, expired leases, opt-out/need changes, suggestion/connection exclusions, complete-scan visibility, deterministic ties, fairness, post-cutoff joins, and no automatic retry after ambiguous SMTP failure.
+
+A local Node benchmark of JSON decode, rank, top-three merge and encode on a 16-person page (29,063 input bytes; 1,000 measured samples) gave 0.236 ms p95, 2.334 ms max. This is NOT a complete invocation benchmark or evidence that deployed Workers CPU stays below 10 ms.
+
+## Not ready for production
+
+1. Throughput/window semantics are unresolved. At one candidate page per minute, a full 50-person pass requires about 200 minutes; 100 people require 700 minutes; 1,000 require 63,000 minutes. This excludes mail and cycle overhead. The intended 11 AM/5 PM digest windows need a bounded dispatch design and a roster-size/delivery-window target. Arbitrarily dropping candidates is not an acceptable fix.
+2. Profile edits during a multi-invocation scan can mix versions. The joined-time cutoff excludes later joins, and changed recipient need/opt-out aborts safely, but a stable candidate snapshot or revision/restart protocol is still needed to preserve single-snapshot ranking. This needs additional database/API work.
+3. Production Workers CPU and complete subrequest accounting, including network/TLS overhead and real Neon responses, still need a non-sending canary. Local wall time/Node CPU cannot certify these limits.
+4. Ties are made deterministic by profile ID. The old SQL SELECT order was unspecified. That explicit tie policy should be reviewed as part of production behavior.
+5. This is not wired to Cron. A future rollout needs reviewed migration/indexes, exclusive replacement of the old digest path, safe batch dispatch, metrics, and rollback. No production schema changes or activation are approved by this package.
+
+The prototype demonstrates a bounded algorithm, not production capacity approval.
