+++ b/snapshot/REVIEW.md
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+# 50-person, three-round Free pilot feasibility
+
+Preparation only. No production resources, code, schema, participants, email or schedules were changed. The source files are a local proof of selected snapshot/idempotency semantics, not a ready-to-activate mailer.
+
+## Result
+
+An immutable per-event/per-slot PostgreSQL snapshot plus Cloudflare Free Queues is a plausible route to dispatch beginning at 11:00, 17:00 and 21:30 IST without the sequential cursor's 200-minute pacing. Cron is UTC: 05:30/11:30 UTC and 16:00 UTC. Queue scheduling and SMTP cannot guarantee inbox receipt at the exact clock instant.
+
+Local fixtures: snapshot creation and pending-ID selection use 2 SQL requests. Each recipient match uses 3 SQL requests and considers all 49 candidates. Fifty fixture mail rows are created without SMTP. Profile edits after capture do not change snapshot matching; live opt-out/need/event state and connections are checked before queueing. Replay and concurrent completion do not duplicate mail. Candidate photos/contact/link fields are excluded. Five snapshot tests plus fourteen cursor tests and the baseline/domain tests total 43 passing tests; TypeScript passes.
+
+A local decode+49-candidate rank+top-three encode benchmark over an 89,024-byte roster gives p95 0.735ms/max 1.114ms. This excludes database-driver, queue-consumer and mail/TLS work and is NOT production CPU certification.
+
+## Recommended dispatch shape
+
+1. Cron writes an immutable event/slot snapshot and a durable pending-job outbox in one statement. It publishes ID-only queue messages. Replays reuse the slot snapshot. Resume unpublished outbox jobs after partial failure. Outbox publication/recovery implementation remains to be built.
+2. Matching consumer batch size1 loads one recipient plus its complete snapshot candidates, excludes prior suggestions/connections, applies the existing keyword scorer, and creates one deduplicated mail row. No AI key or model call. No silent candidate cap; a larger roster must trigger a measured alternate path, not truncated suggestions.
+3. Separate delivery queue messages reference a specific mail ID. An exact-ID conditional pending->sending claim is required, so redelivery cannot send a different pending mail. Current flushOne is not sufficient for that dispatch identity. Keep pre-send live eligibility/quota/connection revalidation. If the process crashes after sending begins, leave the outcome unknown for review; do not retry ambiguous SMTP.
+4. Start delivery concurrency1 to avoid a burst to Gmail; measure rather than promise latency. Consider matching concurrency separately only after canary evidence. Avoid the default ten-message consumer batch.
+5. Store no recipient data in queue payloads or logs. Expire snapshots once the run is complete and the replay/diagnostic window has closed. A concrete retention rule and cleanup must be reviewed. Never copy production secrets into the canary.
+
+## Estimated normal budget
+
+Three rounds x50 people =150 potential match messages/day. One queue stage costs approximately450 read/write/delete operations/day; two stages approximately900, excluding retries, transactional mail, diagnostics and other account usage. Current Free Queues includes10,000 operations/day with24h retention. Snapshot+matching requests are152 SQL requests/round for50 people; a one-message match delivery previously measured14 SQL calls plus SMTP. Separate stages keep these invocations below50 external requests structurally; redirects/driver internals and CPU still need complete runtime measurement.
+
+Gmail's official personal-account documentation warns of limits above500 messages/day, not a delivery guarantee or an allowance confirmed for this sender.150 match emails plus pilot transactional mail can fit arithmetically, but anti-abuse limits/reputation/rate behavior may stop it earlier. Do not probe capacity by sending to real participants.
+
+## Product-cap decision
+
+Current code allows2 recommendation messages and3 total messages/person/day. A third scheduled clock will be skipped for anyone who already got two match emails. Changing match cap to3 but total cap remaining3 means signup/access/connection mail competes with the third digest, and three digests can exhaust a participant's allowance for access/connection mail later that day.
+
+Earlier options considered (superseded by the approved choice below):
+- Retain all existing caps: three dispatch opportunities, at most2 actual match emails/day, transactional mail shares total3.
+- Permit up to3 match emails but retain total3: possible lost/delayed transactional emails, not recommended for an active pilot.
+- Separate up to3 match emails and up to3 transactional emails/day (overall6), preserving the old per-person transactional ceiling but raising overall volume. This supports the desired third eligible digest without consuming the essential-message allowance, but is a product change needing approval. It does not promise an email when there are no new unsuggested matches or the person opted out.
+
+Approved choice: up to3 new-match messages plus up to3 sign-in/connection messages per person/day, overall6. This approval is not implemented in production or the prototype. A schedule is an opportunity, not an email when there are no new matches.
+
+Need an account-wide sender cap/reserve as well, accounting for all events and manual sender use; it must not be inferred from the pilot's50-person target. No cap was changed in preparation.
+
+## Isolated non-sending canary proposal
+
+Fixture-only disposable Free resources were cleared, but testing stopped for the code handoff before the canary ran. The following remains a proposal: create a disposable Free queue/consumer and fixture-only database tables/project or local-mocked remote API; do not attach the production database or SMTP password. Worker contains no SMTP transport and fails if recipient input is not a fixture identifier. Three50-job bursts and explicit duplicate/crash tests measure queue latency, p95/max CPU, total subrequests, memory, cold start, backlog drain, operations and failure behavior. Instrument public output with aggregate non-secret counters only. Use concurrency1 and batch1 initially; test safe matching concurrency independently. Cleanup queue/consumer/fixture data after evidence is saved. A mock SMTP latency can test dispatch behavior but cannot certify Gmail handoff latency or inbox arrival.
+
+Before any live activation: production-ready outbox/exact-ID sender, reviewed add-only migration/indexes and rollback, no-send canary passing conservatively under10ms/50-request limits, user cap decision, exact participant eligibility/opt-out and content/sender scope, and explicit owner authorization for the intended activation and any test sends. Preserve pilot data and keep Cron empty meanwhile.
